1 Purpose of the Revised Instrument and What It
Supersedes
The earlier version of this instrument was written as a two-schedule protocol administered to one participant per schedule. That framing no longer matches how the study
was actually carried out: the interviews were conducted with thirty educators at
each of two Gauteng sites, sixty in total, and the resulting participant-level dataset
(S-01–S-30 for Sandton; SW-01–SW-30 for Soweto) is already coded and aggregated. This
document supersedes the earlier instrument so that the tool, the administered sample,
and the reported distributions describe the same study (see the superseded instrument’s
practical parameters in English Translation and Research Instrument – Semi-Structured
Interviews on Accent, p. 1, and the participant-level data in the interview dataset).
The substantive instrument content — the two matched schedules, the four analytic
themes, and the accent typology — is retained because it was used unchanged in the
field. What changes is the scale of administration and the reporting language around
it:
