2 Sampling Design: Thirty Educators per Site,
Sixty in Total
The administered design is a comparative two-site qualitative study with a purposive
sample of thirty educators at each site (Sandton N = 30; Soweto/Bophelo N = 30;
combined N = 60). Selection followed the logic already stated in the original instrument: deliberate recruitment of teachers positioned on opposite sides of the resource
divide, one group in a high-fee, affluent independent school and one in a fee-free township school. The revised document formalises that logic at the level of the whole cohort
rather than a single representative case per site.
Thirty educators per site was not an arbitrary ceiling. Hennink and Kaiser’s systematic review of empirical saturation tests — 23 articles comprising 17 empirical studies
and 6 statistical modelling studies — found that studies with relatively homogeneous
populations and narrowly defined objectives reached saturation within a narrow band
of 9 to 17 interviews, with code saturation typically arriving at around nine interviews
and meaning saturation requiring 16 to 24 (Hennink & Kaiser, 2022). Because each
site here is internally homogeneous by occupational role and institutional type, a target of 30 per site sits comfortably above the meaning-saturation threshold and leaves
deliberate headroom for the within-site ideological heterogeneity that the data in fact
revealed — the Sandton cohort alone produced three distinct positions, not one.
The claim attached to this sample is bounded and must be stated in those terms:
the sampling is purposive and the findings are not generalisable in the statistical sense,
only transferable to comparable contexts (the original formulation appears at p. 3, line
71 of the superseded instrument). Percentages reported from these sixty participants
are proportions within the sampled cohort; they are not population estimates, and no
confidence interval or significance test should be attached to them.
