3 Instrument Architecture: Two Matched Schedules and Four Analytic Themes
The instrument consists of two matched, individually administered interview schedules,
deliberately constructed as mirror images of one another so that the same themes are
pursued in materially different institutional settings. Part A addresses a teacher at
a lower-resourced, fee-free township school — the Bophelo case in Soweto. Part B
addresses a teacher at a high-fee, affluent school — the Sandton case. Each schedule
runs sequentially through the same four themes: daily teaching reality, accent and
identity, resources and methods, and the meaning of “good English”. Because the
schedules are matched, teacher responses remain directly comparable across the two
sites even though the local conditions that prompted them differ sharply.
The accent content is anchored in a three-point typology — Cultivated, General
and Broad — alongside Black South African English (BSAE), with participants asked
directly which accent carries status in their environment. The schedules also carry the
equity rationale that motivated the study: the Grade 4 reading gap between the bestand worst-performing school cohorts in the system is roughly 80 points, equivalent to
about two years of schooling (van Staden, TIMSS SA, as cited in the instrument). This
figure was the substantive reason for asking teachers how accent norms are handled in
classrooms that sit on opposite sides of the divide.
Interviews run 40 to 60 minutes, ideally in person, and are conducted in English with
an explicit allowance that participants may switch to any other language; the switch
itself is treated as data rather than as a deviation. Scheduled topics, however, are not
a script: interviewers probe where meaning is thickest, compare responses in real time
between the two sites, and record the comparison afterwards rather than during the
exchange, so that no participant is positioned as the object of an ongoing judgement.
4 Coding Pipeline and the Conversion of Narrative
into Percentages
Translating sixty narrative interviews into the site-level percentages reported below required a documented, reproducible pipeline rather than impressionistic counting. The
protocol follows the logic of thematic analysis as a set of recursive phases — familiarisation, initial coding, searching for themes, reviewing themes, defining and naming themes, and reporting (Braun & Clarke, 2006) — and then applies descriptive
quantitizing, the transformation of qualitative material into counts, in the sense developed by Sandelowski, Voils and Knafl (2009) and later systematised in Onwuegbuzie’s
DIME-Driven Model of Quantitizing, which explicitly distinguishes descriptive counting
from inferential or measurement-based quantification. Only descriptive quantitizing is
claimed here: the counts describe the coded corpus, nothing more.
