The five successive phases were applied to all sixty transcripts:
1. Transcription. Verbatim transcription of the semi-structured interviews at both
sites, retaining non-standard dialectal variants and code-switching tokens rather
than normalising them into standard orthography.
2. Unitisation. Decomposition of the transcripts into discrete meaning units —
phrases or propositions expressing a complete pedagogical stance or sociolinguistic
belief — so that one turn in the conversation could yield more than one unit, and
one unit could not be double-counted.
3. Coding. Low-inference descriptive codes applied to each unit, using the code set
actually documented in the dataset: ACC-STD (prescriptive accent remediation),
GRAM-PRE (grammar correction for academic register), GLOB-STD (standard English
as global competitiveness), TRAN-SCAF (translanguaging as scaffolding), RES-CON
and RES-BOOK (material constraint), NEUT (no stated position), among others.
4. Categorisation. Codes clustered into the three analytic categories carried in
the dataset: Standard Language Ideology; Inclusionary/Translanguaging; and
Neutral/No Opinion.
5. Aggregation. Each educator assigned to a single dominant category on the basis
of the whole interview, not a single utterance, and the site count divided by the
site total (30) to produce the reported shares.
Aggregation at the level of the whole interview is the step that protects the percentages from reflecting a stray remark. A teacher who scaffolded one diffcult mathematics
lesson in isiZulu but who described accent correction as an obligation of the job was
coded as Standard, not Inclusion; the reverse applied in the township cohort. This rule
is also why the sites diverge so completely, and it is the rule a reviewer is most entitled
to interrogate.
